  "problem": "Magnus is ordering lunch. He wants to get a starter soup, a drink, a main dish, and a dessert. The menu has $8$ main dishes, $3$ soups, $10$ drinks, and $5$ desserts for him to choose from. How many choices does Magnus have to order a meal?",
  "worked_solution": "Using the **rule of product**, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Number of possible meals} & =\\text{Number of main dishes} \\\\  & \\times \\text{Number of soups} \\\\  & \\times \\text{Number of drinks} \\\\  & \\times \\text{Number of desserts} \\\\  & =8\\times 3\\times 10\\times 5 \\\\  & =1\\,200.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Magnus has $1\\,200$ choices to order a meal.",
